  "pride_metadata": {
    "project": {
      "accession": "PXD044201",
      "title": "Plasma Proteomic Characterization of Colorectal Cancer Patients with FOLFOX Chemotherapy",
      "additionalAttributes": [],
      "projectDescription": "Colorectal Cancer (CRC) is one of most common cancers in the world and a main treatment in postoperative chemotherapy is oxaliplatin and fluorouracil (FOLFOX), But the effect is different among CRC patients. In this study, LC-MS/MS strategy was used to profile the plasma proteome in FOLFOX benefit and futile group. As a result, a panel of plasma proteins by machine learning from our data was verified for a possible prediction tool in postdiagnosis.",
      "sampleProcessingProtocol": "Proteome Sample Preparation  For plasma proteome profiling, 1 \u03bcL plasma of each sample were diluted by D-hanks buffer then prepared by SISPROT Kit (BayOmics, China) followed by manufacture\u2019s protocol. Whole proteome sample preparation including reduction alkylation and digestion were done in this kit and signal procedure costed 3h in total. The produced peptides could then be injected for LC-MS/MS injection, after reconstitution by 0.1 x indexed retention time (iRT, Biognosys, Switzerland) in the LC mobile phase A consists of 0.1% formic acid in water.  LC-MS/MS Data Acquisition  A data-independent acquisition (DIA) method was adopted on Orbitrap Exploris 240 mass spectrometer coupled with Ultimate 3000 liquid chromatography (ThermoFisher, USA). Peptide samples were separated by self-packed analytical column (100 \u03bcm x 20cm, 1.9 \u03bcm C18) at a flow rate of 500 nL/min. The mobile phase B consists of 0.1% formic acid in acetonitrile. A 65 min gradient set as: 0\u20132 min, 4-10% B; 2\u201352 min, 10\u201328% B; 52\u201362min, 28\u201345% B, 62-64min, 45-99% and last 1min continues 99%B. For MS instrument settings, the mass range of MS1 was set as 400\u20131200 m/z at the resolution of 6,000 with 300% automatic gain control (AGC) and auto maximum injection time mode. For the DIA setting, mass range of 400\u20131200 m/z was equally divided into 32 continuous windows MS2 scans at 30,000 resolution with the 54 ms max injection time and 1000% AGC of 1E6. Stepped normalized collision energy of MS2 was distributed to 28, 32, and 36.",
      "dataProcessingProtocol": "Data analysis and statistics Raw data were analyzed by Spectronaut softeware (v16.0, Biognosys, Switzerland) with the directDIA mode. Original expression intensities of proteins were normalized by median value of each sample. Metaboanalyst was used for statistical analysis including log10 transformation, differential analysis, t-test and the FDR calculation. A machine learning method, RandomForest, was used to identify plasma proteome futures and established a protein panel for prediction of curative effect. R package pROC was used for mode evaluation. The outer resampling method with cross-validation consists of 3-fold and repeated 5 times, was processed for variables selection and prediction power were evaluated by ROC analysis.   Biomarker combination validation by PRM quantification A new cohort of plasma samples were collected and prepared in the same way as the discovery cohort as described, and analyzed by parallel reaction monitoring (PRM), a targeted quantification method of orbitrap MS. All the validation data were acquired in the Orbitrap Exploris 240 mass spectrometer, the LC setting are same with the precious discover proteome profiling. For the PRM MS setting, 120K resolution for full scan was adopted with a scan range 400-1200 and maximum injection time 100ms. Targeted MS2 set an isolation window with m/z 0.7 at a resolution 45K and maximum injection time 150ms coupled AGC 100% of 1E5. Data analysis, including peak filtration and area calculation, was processed by skyline software (v21.2).",
      "projectTags": [],
      "keywords": [
        "Human plasma proteome; sisprot; lc-ms/ms; random forest; prm validation"
      ],
